    <h2>1. The 6-Square-Foot Rule (NFPA 211 &amp; IRC R1001.9)</h2>
    <p>
      The National Fire Protection Association (NFPA 211) and International Residential Code (IRC Section R1001.9) divide masonry fireplace hearth extensions into two clear regulatory tiers based on the surface area of the firebox opening:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Small Fireboxes (&lt; 6.0 sq ft opening area):</strong> The hearth extension must extend at least <strong>16 inches in front of</strong> the firebox opening, and at least <strong>8 inches beyond each side</strong> of the opening.</li>
      <li><strong>Large Fireboxes (&ge; 6.0 sq ft opening area):</strong> The hearth extension must extend at least <strong>20 inches in front of</strong> the opening, and at least <strong>12 inches beyond each side</strong> of the opening.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-600 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-blue-900 my-4">
      Example Calculation:<br/>
      • Firebox Opening Width = 36 inches (3.0 ft)<br/>
      • Firebox Opening Height = 26 inches (2.17 ft)<br/>
      • Opening Area = 3.0 ft x 2.17 ft = 6.51 sq ft<br/>
      • Result: Because 6.51 &ge; 6.0 sq ft, this fireplace LEGALLY REQUIRES a 20-inch front extension and 12-inch side extensions!
    </div>

