    <h2>3. Structural Hearth Slab Construction (No Wood Allowed Below)</h2>
    <p>
      Under IRC Section R1001.9.1, hearth extensions must be constructed of approved non-combustible materials and must be supported by masonry or reinforced concrete. The concrete slab forming the hearth extension must be a minimum of <strong>4 inches in solid thickness</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      In modern wood-framed residential homes, builders achieve this by cantilevering structural concrete over corrugated metal stay-form pans supported by doubled floor joists. No combustible wood framing (including subfloor plywood or 2x10 joists) can touch or run directly underneath the 4-inch concrete hearth slab.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Combustible Wood Mantel Clearances (IRC R1001.11)</h2>
    <p>
      Installing a rustic timber wood mantel too close to the fireplace opening is a frequent cause of home fires. The International Residential Code enforces the <strong>mantel projection rule</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Absolute 6-Inch Rule:</strong> All combustible materials (wood trim, corbels, mantels) must be located a minimum of <strong>6 inches</strong> away from the fireplace opening perimeter.</li>
      <li><strong>The Projection Ratio:</strong> For any combustible mantel located between 6 inches and 12 inches from the opening, the mantel may not project more than 1/8 inch for each 1 inch of clearance. For example, a mantel located 10 inches above the opening may project a maximum of 1-1/4 inches from the wall surface.</li>
      <li><strong>Deep Mantels:</strong> A full 8-inch or 10-inch deep timber mantel shelf must be mounted at least <strong>12 inches (and often 18 to 24 inches)</strong> above the top of the firebox opening.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

