    title="The Raised Hearth Drop-Off Code: Watch Out for Tripping Hazards"
    note="When homeowners construct a raised hearth (a stone platform elevated 12 to 18 inches off the living room floor that doubles as a bench), building officials evaluate it under staircase and guardrail safety rules as well as fire safety. If the hearth platform is elevated more than 30 inches off the floor, code technically requires a guardrail (which ruins the aesthetics). More importantly, the hearth extension dimensions apply to the RAISED SURFACE. If you build a raised bench that extends 16 inches out, but place a wood-burning fireplace insert with a door that swings open 14 inches, a popping spark will jump clean over the raised bench and land on your hardwood floor below! When building a raised hearth, always extend the non-combustible cap at least 20 to 24 inches in depth so embers cannot bounce onto surrounding carpet or hardwood."
